-C7). Creating a long wall of my pieces here is strategically risky because it gives the opponent many anchors to play into the empty B-row on their next turn.\n- **(G, 7)**: This is an X-square move. Usually risky, but since I own the adjacent corner (H8), it is completely safe. It captures G6, making the entire G-row mine (`*`). It consolidates a massive block of stable territory at the bottom of the board (Rows G and H) without opening up dangerous new frontiers for the opponent.\n\nReason: I chose (G, 7) because it capitalizes on my ownership of the H8 corner to safely secure the entire G-row. This maximizes my stable piece count and restricts the opponent's counter-play, whereas moves like (C, 8) would grant the opponent too much mobility by flipping the C-row wall.\n\nAnswer: (G, 7)"}
