om moving the edge or the corners. I want to build a stable wall, secure the edge, and push my opponent into the upper corner. I have found an advantage and will not give it up. I believe this will set me on the path to winning. I have analyzed all the options and have chosen what gives me the best chance.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the late stages with 16 empty squares remaining. I have a strong presence in the bottom rows (G, H) and the left edge. The opponent occupies the center and the majority of Row C. The primary empty region is the top two rows (A and B). I have a specific opportunity to play at (C, 8), which is the last empty square in Row C.\n\nEvaluation: The move at (C, 8) is exceptionally strong for several reasons:\n1.  **Flipping Power:** It captures the entire horizontal line of opponent pieces from C2 to C7, as well as a diagonal line (D7, E6, F5). This results in a significant shift in piece count and board control.\n2.  **Edge Stability:** By playing (C, 8), I secure the entire C-row (C1 is already mine). This creates a solid "wall" of my pieces that acts as a barrier, making it difficult for the opponent to flank my position from the top.\n3.  **Mobility Restriction:** Currently, the opponent could potentially play at B8 by anchoring off C7. By flipping C7 to my color, I deny this move. Furthermore, since the column C8-H8 will be entirely mine, the opponent cannot easily take the adjacent A8 corner (they lack the necessary anchors).\n4.  **Forcing Moves:** With the C-row secured, the opponent is forced to play into the empty A and B rows (like B3, B5, or B6) without the support of the C-row pieces, effectively allowing me to play "quiet" moves or counter-attack their extensions.\n\nReason: I chose (C, 8) because it secures a stable edge, denies the opponent access to the top-right area (specifically B8 and A8), and forces the opponent into a disadvantageous position in the remaining open space.\n\nAnswer: (C, 8)'}
