ket on the right (A6, B6). The opponent has very limited mobility.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Move (A, 2): Valid, but playing to a C-square (adjacent to corner) while the corner A1 is empty can be risky, potentially giving the opponent access to A1 via a sandwich with A3.\n- Move (B, 2): An X-square move. While it flips pieces, it often invites the opponent to the corner. However, calculating the lines shows it might not give A1 immediately, but it is less structurally sound than edge moves.\n- Move (B, 6): Flips C6, D6. It forces the opponent to respond in the isolated right pocket (likely A6). However, giving up A6 creates a stable opponent piece on the edge that is hard to flip back.\n- Move (A, 4): Flips B3. This is an edge move that connects to my existing pieces at A5, A7, A8. If the opponent responds by taking A3, I can recapture A3 by playing A2 later (sandwiching A3 between A2 and A4). This maintains control of the edge.\n- Move (A, 3): Similar to A4, but leaves A4 empty. If the opponent takes A4, I cannot easily recapture it as I lack a sandwiching piece at A2/A3/A5 in the correct configuration.\n\nReason: Move (A, 4) is the most prudent choice. It extends my edge control securely. Unlike (B, 6) or (A, 3), it ensures that if the opponent tries to split the edge (by playing A3), I have a guaranteed counter-move (A2) to flip their piece back and consolidate the entire row. It also restricts the opponent's mobility without exposing the critical A1 corner.\n\nAnswer: (A, 4)"}
